{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Successor owner's duty over security deposit and interest","requirement":"When a rental property is sold or transferred, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit plus accrued interest and is responsible for investing it, giving all notices, paying interest and returning the deposit with earnings.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental property in New Jersey that is sold or transferred.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Applies when the property is sold or transferred"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"If the property is sold or transferred it is the duty of the new owner to obtain the security deposit, plus accrued interest on the tenant’s deposit, that was collected by the former owner.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalty for withholding deposit of an assisted tenant","requirement":"A landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit from a tenant receiving state or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance, may be liable for a civil penalty of $500 to $2,000 for each offense.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who receive state or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Tenants receiving governmental financial assistance"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, enforced by summary proceedings; the state entity that made the deposits is entitled to the penalty amounts.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any Stat e or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance , may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Criminal penalty for unlawful use of deposit monies","requirement":"A person who unlawfully uses security deposit monies may be charged as a disorderly person and may be fined at least $200, imprisoned for up to 30 days, or both.","key_value":"$200 fine or 30 days' imprisonment","coverage_conditions":"Security deposit monies held for residential tenancies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Disorderly person charge with a fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-25","quoted_span":"Any person who unlawfully uses security deposit monies may be c riminally charged as a disorderly person and may be subject to a fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days, or both (N.J.S.A. 46:8-25).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination - rental discrimination and source of lawful income","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in the rental of housing based on protected characteristics, including source of lawful income, and must give equal treatment in rental housing.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex (familial status).","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Exceptions for owner-occupied two-family dwellings and owner- or resident-occupied single homes"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, as it pertains to familial status.","penalty":"Landlords who violate the LAD are subject to fines and penalties up to $10,000 for a first offense (N.J.S.A. 10:5-14.1a(a)); monetary damages and an order to rent the unit to the complainant may be imposed.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a residential dwelling only by tenant consent or through the legal eviction process, must have good cause, and must serve a notice to quit (except for nonpayment of rent) before filing suit.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"The Act protects blameless tenants from eviction; a landlord of a dwelling unit used as a residence may recover possession only by consent or through the legal eviction process.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Dwelling units used as a residence"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act limits on rent increases","requirement":"A rent increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with any municipal rent control ordinance; before evicting for nonpayment of an increased rent the landlord must serve a valid notice to quit and notice of rent increase.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies covered by the Anti-Eviction Act; increases are also subject to municipal rent control or rent leveling ordinances where they exist.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental units; municipal ordinances may govern increases"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":"State law leaves rent control to municipalities; the increase must comply with any municipal rent control ordinance.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law 30-year rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from local rent control ordinances for 30 years after construction of the building is completed. Rents subsidized by government funding may also be exempt.","key_value":"30 years exemption from local rent control","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; the exemption runs for 30 years following completion of construction. Rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; 30 years from completion of construction"},"exemptions":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"Exempts covered buildings from local rent control ordinances.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Ban on conversion-related rent increases","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units, a landlord may not increase rents to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, regardless of protected-tenancy eligibility.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Rents may be increased to cover the cost of new services or amenities.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
